# Line endings: LF everywhere, which is what the repository already stores.
#
# .editorconfig used to demand CRLF for *.cs while nothing enforced it on checkout, so on Linux
# the files arrived as LF and `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` objected to every line of every
# file. That is why CI's lint step could never pass.
#
# Handing CRLF to the working tree fixes a fresh clone but not a reused one: git only converts
# line endings when it actually writes a file, so a long-lived checkout - such as a self-hosted
# runner's _work directory - keeps whatever bytes it already had, and the tree ends up mixed.
# Matching .editorconfig to the LF that git stores removes the failure mode instead of papering
# over it, and *.cs was the last holdout: js, ts and svelte were already lf.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.cs text eol=lf

# Verify snapshot files. VerifyChecks.Run enforces these once a .gitattributes exists, and the
# snapshots must stay LF regardless of the C# rule above or every one of them would churn.
*.verified.txt text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
*.verified.xml text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8
*.verified.json text eol=lf working-tree-encoding=UTF-8

# Windows-only tooling that genuinely wants CRLF on disk.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
